  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n- $T$ is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,\u27fa\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
